-- Sovietski: the SU-90 Iron Forge mobile cyborg hospital and the SN-247 Torrent fast-attack gunboat with its medium torpedoes (printed 204-207).
-- 2 vehicle rows, with 25 locations and 8 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~392-sovietski-v7.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('su-90-iron-forge', 'SU-90 Iron Forge Mobile Cyborg Hospital', 'rifts', 'vehicle', 'Six: a Tank Commander, a Tank Driver, two Tank Specialists and two gunners.', 'Hospital staff: a Head of Surgery (a level 1D4+6 Cyber-Doc or Vedmak holding a Colonel''s rank, who sets the order of every procedure), three Body Fixers, six Cyber-Docs, six Vedmak Cyber-Docs, 12 nurses, two chemists or lab techs, four field medics and four Heavy Machines for lifting. Patients: up to 24 bedridden and 24-30 walking, with room for 30 more in an emergency; a Heavy Machine counts as two human-sized patients and a Superheavy Machine as four.', '80 mph (128 km) top; 60 mph (96 km) off-road; 40 mph (64 km) cruising.', 'None.', 'Cannot go underwater; fords water up to 12 feet (3.7 m) deep with every outside door shut.', 'Height 31 feet (9.4 m), width 33 feet (10 m), length 155 feet (47.2 m).', '210 tons fully loaded', 320, 1, NULL, NULL, 'Not available: no Iron Forge has ever reached the Black Market. A new, undamaged one would fetch 40 to 60 million credits there; Warlords want them badly and have tried more than once to seize one.', 'A huge wheeled field hospital built because Sovietski soldiers lean so heavily on bionics: a cyborg whose limb or weapon fails at the front may be far from any clinic, folk medicine cannot fix machine parts, magic healing does not mix with them, and a Vedmak Cyber-Doc carries only so many spares. The Iron Forge parks behind the lines and the wounded are brought to it to be repaired, refitted with cybernetic or bionic eyes, organs and limbs, and sent back to the fight without a trip home. Partial bionic rebuilding is done here; full conversion of a soldier into a Light or Heavy Machine cannot be done here. Bay doors in the belly open to hoist big, broken Heavy and Superheavy Machines straight into the main bay, and a flat rear deck with four cranes (two large, two small) lifts damaged cyborgs aboard for assessment, stabilizing and triage. Each large crane carries a Jaws of Life pry bar, a laser torch and a winch with a tow cable that takes hooks, straps, nets or a platform; a large crane lifts 10 tons, a small one five. A ram prow at the front pushes through wreckage and disabled vehicles to reach the injured; it can knock targets aside (the book refers to the Road Hammer for ramming stats), though doing so with patients aboard is unwise. Medical fit: eight bionic surgical bays and six smaller suites for cybernetic implants and simple bionic work such as eyes and light limbs, with X-ray, EKG, EEG, MRI/CT and full body scanning, a clean room, a quarantine room, a refrigerated laboratory, drug, cybernetics and bionics dispensaries, medical waste disposal and supply bays. Also a doctors'' lounge, four sleeping bays of four bunks each, two showers and a kitchen. Stores: a broad stock of cybernetic and bionic parts, 40 sets of Light Machine replacement limbs and 20 of Heavy, common bionic weapon systems, implants, eyes and organs, 72 of each nano-medical system, 10 portable computers, two portable laboratories, six language translators, five Portable Scan Dihilators, five portable Bio-Scan and Bio-Lab units, and five bare Light Machine cyborg frames plus two Heavy. Other supplies: 10 days of rations for crew and staff, 15,000 gallons of water, an energy rifle for every person aboard, 8 suits of Red Star body armor, five jet packs, 12 communicators, a field radio and two snowmobiles. Losing hover thrusters costs 10% speed each; losing half the wheels and both thrusters on one side halves speed, with -20% piloting and -2 to dodge, and further losses take another 20%. With every wheel and thruster gone on one side it drags along at 20 mph (32 km) at most, surgery becomes impossible, patients are -20% to save vs coma/death, and piloting is -40% and -5 to dodge; such a hospital should wait for repairs or move its people to another. Nuclear, 20-year average life. Standard Sovietski vehicle sensors.', 'Rifts World Book 36: Sovietski p.204-205'),
  ('sn-247-torrent', 'SN-247 Torrent Fast-Attack Gunboat', 'rifts', 'ship', 'Five: a captain/pilot, a first mate/co-pilot, a navigation, sensor and communications officer, and two gunners.', 'Up to eight extra troops (soldiers or Light Machines), six Heavy Machines squeezed in tight, or three Superheavy Machines; the book then says speed drops by 10%, a clause that may cover only the Superheavy load.', 'None.', 'None.', '55 mph (88 km) cruising; 100 mph (160 km) with the rear thrusters lit, used for chases down straight river stretches and across open lakes and seas, at -20% to the piloting skill while they burn. Cannot submerge.', 'Height 16 feet (4.9 m), width 19 feet (5.8 m), length 68 feet (20.7 m).', '36 tons', 310, 1, NULL, 42000000, '42 million credits on the Black Market for a new, undamaged boat; extremely rare.', 'A small, nimble patrol gunboat working Russia''s rivers, lakes and other waterways, most often off the Sovietski coast, and sent as well on long reconnaissance trips beyond Sovietski lands. Boosters give it a burst of straight-line speed for running down pirates and sea monsters, and it also escorts freighters and guards Sovietski ports. New Moscow and the Volga River Fortress keep flotillas of them at their river docks, and Kaliningrad and New Leningrad have 20 each, with more being built. Armament: two heavy rail gun turrets forward, a dual ion turret aft, a mini-missile turret on the cabin roof and two medium torpedo tubes. A small crew runs it, and the troop space takes a boarding party; heavier cyborgs are sometimes brought along for extra punch against big monsters or well-armed pirates like the Horune. Cargo: the hold, which is also the troop bay, carries up to 60 days of supplies for the crew when no troops are aboard, food and fresh water included, with a locker per crew member holding a first aid kit, a suit of body armor, a rifle, a side arm and 4 clips. A separate weapons locker holds 32 spare mini-missiles, two jet packs, and 4,000 reload rounds per rail gun. A small cabin has four bunks, a seating area, a kitchenette and a lavatory. If the main body is lost the boat sinks in 3D6 minutes; its two inflatable rafts carry the crew (printed as all four crew members) and Light-Machine-size troops but not Heavy or Superheavy Machines, which ride at their own risk, since a boat big enough for them would cost the gunboat 25% of its speed. Nuclear, 15-year average life. Standard Sovietski vehicle sensors plus: life support (air circulation and scrubbing with gas filtration and a toxin alarm; the pilot compartment seals, with eight hours of oxygen and air recycling good for about 14-18 days); an electronic counter-measure system that jams every enemy transmission within a 20 mile (32 km) radius and, tied to the radar and combat computer, traces enemy broadcasts on jammed channels and homing beacons to their rough location; and sonar that finds submerged targets out to 30 miles (48 km), identifying and logging each contact, +10% to Sensory Equipment for a trained operator.', 'Rifts World Book 36: Sovietski p.205-207');

